ther rises nor falls in the capillary. The contact angle may be $0^{\\circ}$.\nStatement II : The contact angle between a solid and a liquid is a property of the material of the solid and liquid as well.\nIn the light of the above statement, choose the correct answer from the options given below."}, "options": [{"id": "a093d6", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a891648d"}, {"id": "8646b9", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a891648e"}, {"id": "685c2b", "text": "Statement I is false but Statement II is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a891648f"}, {"id": "23027d", "text": "Statement I is true and Statement II is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916490"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916491", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5648"], "question": {"text": "Two spherical soap bubbles of radii <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> in vacuum combine under isothermal conditions. The resulting bubble has a radius equal to:"}, "options": [{"id": "0eb49c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916492"}, {"id": "591f11", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916493"}, {"id": "992427", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916494"}, {"id": "fd2877", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916495"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916496", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8ed2"], "question": {"text": "Two water drops each of radius 'r' coalesce to from a bigger drop. If ' T ' is the surface tension, the surface energy released in this process is :"}, "options": [{"id": "633480", "text": "$4 \\pi \\mathrm{r}^2 \\mathrm{~T}\\left[2-2^{\\frac{2}{3}}\\right]$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916497"}, {"id": "46d032", "text": "$4 \\pi r^2 \\mathrm{~T}\\left[2-2^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\right]$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916498"}, {"id": "b450fe", "text": "$4 \\pi r^2 \\mathrm{~T}[1+\\sqrt{2}]$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916499"}, {"id": "4eeec5", "text": "$4 \\pi r^2 T[\\sqrt{2}-1]$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a891649a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a891649b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b51"], "question": {"text": "A capillary tube made of glass of radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> is dipped vertically in a beaker filled with methylene iodide (surface tension&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>05</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>667</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>) which rises to height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></math> in the tube. It is observed that the two tangents drawn from observed that the two tangents drawn from liquid-glass interfaces (from opp. sides of the capillary) make an angle of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#186;</mo></math> with one another. Then&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></math> is close to (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>)"}, "options": [{"id": "c92e11", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>049</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a891649c"}, {"id": "b79460", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>087</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a891649d"}, {"id": "1108cf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>137</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a891649e"}, {"id": "a0ac6d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>172</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a891649f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164a0", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5648"], "question": {"text": "The excess pressure inside a soap bubble is thrice the excess pressure inside a second soap bubble. The ratio between the volume of the first and the second bubble is:"}, "options": [{"id": "91f993", "text": "$1: 9$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164a1"}, {"id": "8522b3", "text": "$1: 3$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164a2"}, {"id": "c73431", "text": "$1: 27$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164a3"}, {"id": "2a4301", "text": "$1: 81$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164a4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164a5", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8ed2"], "question": {"text": "A mercury drop of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> is broken into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>125</mn></math> equal size droplets. Surface tension of mercury is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> . The gain in surface energy is:"}, "options": [{"id": "a284ef", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>26</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164a6"}, {"id": "cde321", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>28</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164a7"}, {"id": "79e30d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>17</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164a8"}, {"id": "58f4ff", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164a9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164aa", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b51"], "question": {"text": "The ratio of surface tensions of mercury and water is given to be <math><mn>7.5</mn></math>, while the ratio of their densities is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>13.6</mn></math>.&nbsp;Their contact angles, with glass, are close to <math><mn>135</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, respectively. If it is observed that mercury gets depressed by an amount <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math> in a capillary tube of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>, while water rises by the same amount <math><mi>h</mi></math> in a capillary tube of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>, then&nbsp;the ratio&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></math>&nbsp;is close to"}, "options": [{"id": "952241", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164ab"}, {"id": "93bc0b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164ac"}, {"id": "da5843", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164ad"}, {"id": "b6bd13", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164ae"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164af", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5648"], "question": {"text": "Pressure inside two soap bubbles are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn></math> atmosphere, respectively. The ratio of their&nbsp;volumes is :"}, "options": [{"id": "6e725a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164b0"}, {"id": "0fc2a9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164b1"}, {"id": "af28bf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164b2"}, {"id": "c5cb22", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164b3"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164b4", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8ed2"], "question": {"text": "A large number of water drops, each of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>, combine to have a drop of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>. If the surface tension is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math> and mechanical equivalent of heat is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>J</mi></math>, the rise in heat energy per unit volume will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "dee152", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164b5"}, {"id": "319df0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164b6"}, {"id": "7d247d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>R</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164b7"}, {"id": "631ca1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>R</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164b8"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:29:23.862Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:29:23.862Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916552"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916550", "title": "Physics Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164f0", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e567de499d1e49588f9cd5"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa01c"], "question": {"text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FJAgYlTgYhdnPzhaxEGcK_KOubC2E7v_7dAvAflCebsQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/>\nA hydraulic press containing water has two arms with diameters as mentioned in the figure. A force of $10 \\mathrm{~N}$ is applied on the surface of water in the thinner arm. The force required to be applied on the surface of water in the thicker arm to maintain equilibrium of water is _____N."}, "options": [{"id": "b85964", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164f1"}, {"id": "d6dd1c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164f2"}, {"id": "c2f91a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164f3"}, {"id": "4e9093", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164f4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164f5", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4782"], "concepts": ["6162faf96ed13040ec8499bd"], "question": {"text": "<p>Consider a water tank as shown in the figure.&nbsp;It&#39;s cross-sectional area is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. The tank has&nbsp;an opening <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> near the bottom whose cross-section area is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. A load of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>24</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> is applied on the water at the top when the height of the water level is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> above the bottom, the&nbsp;velocity of water coming out the opening <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math>, to the nearest integer,&nbsp;is&nbsp;___ .[Take the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi></math> to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>]</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F8287be9f-a647-4a24-ae00-d04694fadf85-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 210px; height: 203px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "6f4ac5", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164f6"}, {"id": "c561ce", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164f7"}, {"id": "e0aa9a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164f8"}, {"id": "77e171", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164f9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164fa", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5648"], "question": {"text": "A spherical soap bubble of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> is formed inside another spherical soap bubble of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. If the internal pressure of the smaller bubble of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> in the above system is equal to the internal pressure of the another single soap bubble of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> is _____"}, "options": [{"id": "dfa262", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164fb"}, {"id": "431a0d", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164fc"}, {"id": "1baa98", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164fd"}, {"id": "9a5591", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164fe"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a89164ff", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb9e66f9ce3f243b52e9"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52bc"], "question": {"text": "<p>A spherical ball of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>&nbsp;and density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>cc</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> is dropped in glycerine of coefficient of viscosity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn></math> poise and density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>cc</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. Viscous force on the ball when it attains constant velocity is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3696</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is</p><p>(Given,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>22</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac></math>)</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "3d765f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916500"}, {"id": "a5fc0c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916501"}, {"id": "6129dc", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916502"}, {"id": "2c98f3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916503"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f43c677dc36a8916504", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8ed2"], "question": {"text": "A thin transparent film with refractive index 1.4 , is held on circular ring of radius 1.8 cm . The fluid in the film evaporates such that transmission through the film at wavelength 560 nm goes to a minimum every 12 seconds. Assuming that the film is flat on its tw